The UN Declaration of Human Rights (Article 12) recognises privacy as a fundamental right; 130+ countries enshrine it in their constitutions.
Surveillance chilling effects are real: a 2016 study found Wikipedia page views of terrorism-related topics fell 30% after Snowden's NSA revelations — people avoid legitimate research when watched.
Domestic abuse survivors, political dissidents, whistleblowers, and LGBTQ+ people in hostile environments depend on privacy as a literal safety requirement, not a lifestyle preference.
